---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { evaluation, experimentById, progress } from '../lib/data';
import { pct } from '../lib/format';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const fam = (id: string, name: string) => experimentById(id)?.families[name]?.exact ?? null;
const grp = (id: string, group: string) => experimentById(id)?.groups[group]?.exact ?? null;

const train = evaluation.results.train;

// Earlier float32 experiments, copied from experiments/*.json (seed 0; within 1% relative error counted as correct).
const phases = [
  {
    tag: 'Attempt 1',
    title: 'A small recurrent network on decimal numbers',
    body: 'Inputs and outputs were float32 numbers. A recurrent network read the 999 examples and had to output c, counted correct within 1% relative error. After 1,000 training updates it was essentially never right.',
    rows: [
      ['add', '1.2%'],
      ['sub', '1.0%'],
      ['mul', '0%'],
      ['div', '0%'],
    ],
    file: 'fixed999-seed0-eval.json',
    verdict: 'Fails',
  },
  {
    tag: 'Attempt 2',
    title: 'Curriculum, memory slots, more training',
    body: 'Small numbers first, several memory slots, 3,000 updates and 133K parameters. Better, but just copying the c of the closest example still did better on every basic relation.',
    rows: [
      ['add', '30.9% (closest example 42.6%)'],
      ['sub', '11.7% (14.1%)'],
      ['mul', '16.4% (19.5%)'],
      ['div', '1.6% (16.4%)'],
    ],
    file: 'curriculum-long-m4-eval.json',
    verdict: 'Below the closest-example baseline',
  },
  {
    tag: 'Attempt 3',
    title: 'A built-in curve fitter (residual loop)',
    body: 'Replaced the network with a fixed curve-fitting loop (conjugate gradient) over a hand-chosen set of features, with 61 trainable parameters. It passed 90% on all four operations — but an untrained copy scored the same. The fitter did the work, not learning. And “within 1%” is still a guess, not an answer.',
    rows: [
      ['add', '99.7% (untrained 99.7%)'],
      ['sub', '99.2% (99.4%)'],
      ['mul', '94.5% (94.7%)'],
      ['div', '97.4% (97.7%)'],
    ],
    file: 'residual-pilot-basic-seed4000{0,1,2}.json (pooled)',
    verdict: 'High score, but not learned',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Changelog" description="Every experiment so far, from decimal regression to exact bit-level relation inference at 32 bits, including what did not work.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Changelog</p>
  <h1>From fuzzy decimals to exact bits</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The goal never changed: given exactly 999 examples of an unknown relation <code>c = f(a, b)</code> and one new
    <code>(a, b)</code>, produce <code>c</code> — with no operation name, no menu of formulas, and no retraining at
    answer time. What changed was how numbers are shown to the model, and what counts as right.
  </p>

  <ol class="timeline">
    {
      phases.map((p) => (
        <li class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow">{p.tag} · float32 · within 1% counts as right</p>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.body}</p>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              {p.rows.map(([op, v]) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{op}</code>
                  </td>
                  <td class="num">{v}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="small muted">
            {p.verdict} · <code>experiments/{p.file}</code>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }

    <li class="card pivot">
      <p class="eyebrow">The switch · 8-bit integers · all bits right or wrong</p>
      <h3>Write numbers as bits, score only exact answers</h3>
      <p>
        Instead of one decimal number, each of <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code> becomes 8 bits, and the
        model predicts each bit of <code>c</code> as 0 or 1. Results wrap at 256. An answer is right only when all 8 bits
        are. This trades “close enough” for a yes-or-no answer, and the model can say how sure it is about each bit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before building anything, a quick test with no learning asked whether 999 examples are even enough. A hand-written
        lookup walks the bits from lowest to highest. At bit <code>k</code> it keeps the examples whose bits at
        <code>k</code> and at the previous position match the question, and copies their <code>c<sub>k</sub></code>:
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>relation</th>
              <th class="num">closest example</th>
              <th class="num">lookup, looking back 1 bit</th>
              <th class="num">looking back 2 bits</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              ['add', 'sub', 'xor', 'a+2b', 'max'].map((n) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{n}</code>
                  </td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(train[n]?.nearest)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(Math.max(train[n]?.window1L ?? 0, train[n]?.window1H ?? 0))}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(Math.max(train[n]?.window2L ?? 0, train[n]?.window2H ?? 0))}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Three lessons: <strong>copying the closest example is useless with bits</strong> (2–5% for add/sub); <strong
          >going bit by bit, remembering just the previous position, is exact</strong
        > for carry-style relations; and <strong>looking further back fails</strong> because 999 examples don't cover all
        the combinations. So the model has to learn a short summary of the bits so far — like a carry — and that is
        what its loop does.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Bit-serial model · first learned model</p>
      <h3>A learned loop over bit positions, plus a lookup over the examples</h3>
      <p>
        One shared recurrent network reads every example one bit at a time; at each bit the question looks up the
        examples in the same situation; one pass runs from the lowest bit, one from the highest. 100% exact on all 12
        named relations it trained on — but only {pct(fam('serial', 'min'))} on <code>min</code>, the mirror of a trained
        idea, because it could not decide which direction to read. <a href={`${base}/first-model/`}>Inside this model →</a>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">All bits at once · loop on the answer</p>
      <h3>Drop the reading order, keep a loop on the answer</h3>
      <p>
        Each example becomes one vector from all 24 of its bits, fixed for the whole run. The question keeps a guess of
        all 8 answer bits and corrects it every round. Never-seen relations improved: <code>3a</code>
        {pct(fam('serial', '3a'))} → {pct(fam('loop', '3a'))},
        <code>min</code> {`${pct(fam('serial', 'min'))} → ${pct(fam('loop', 'min'))}`}, and confidence became far more
        useful.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Multiplication · multiplicative units</p>
      <h3>Networks mostly add; give them a way to multiply</h3>
      <p>
        Trained on <code>a*b</code> alone, the plain loop stalls at {pct(fam('mulonly-loop', 'a*b'))}. Adding bilinear
        inputs (two projections multiplied) and gated feed-forward layers lifts it to <strong
          >{pct(fam('mulonly-mloop', 'a*b'))}</strong
        >. Among 14 relations, though, multiplication got only 1/14 of
        training and reached {pct(fam('mul-mloop', 'a*b'))}. Two other designs were dead ends: letting the 999 example
        tokens act as working memory was too slow on a 4 GB GPU, and inferring the weights of a tiny calculator with a
        periodic (cosine) readout could represent multiplication exactly but could not be trained.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Adaptive sampling · random expressions</p>
      <h3>Spend training where the model is still wrong</h3>
      <p>
        Drawing relations in proportion to how often they are still wrong fixed the starvation: <code>a*b</code>
        {pct(fam('mix', 'a*b'))} inside mixed training, every earlier relation 98–100% (<code>min</code>
        {pct(fam('mix', 'min'))}). Random 1–3 operation expressions joined training, with eight compositions never
        generated — checked by behaviour, so rewrites cannot leak. Those hidden compositions reached
        <strong>{pct(grp('mix', 'hidden'))}</strong> on average (copying the closest example: about 9%).
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Product, then another step · a computation limit</p>
      <h3>It multiplies, it adds — but not one after the other</h3>
      <p>
        <code>a*b+a</code> stayed near {pct(fam('mix2', 'a*b+a'))}: the low four bits right, the high bits wrong —
        carries through an intermediate result. More multi-step training data lifted hidden compositions to
        {pct(grp('mix2', 'hidden'))} but not this; a round counter did not help either. Trained on nothing but four such
        relations, the model still reached only {pct(fam('nearonly', 'a*b+b'))}: the loop has no place to hold an
        intermediate result.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Loops and the difficulty ladder</p>
      <h3>Functions defined by loops, measured on a ladder</h3>
      <p>
        Training now includes loops whose repeat count comes from <code>b</code> (sums of sequences, repeated
        application, Fibonacci-like recurrences, bit counting), and the tests are a ladder from 2-operation expressions to
        hidden loop bodies run longer than ever trained. The model may either execute the loop or find a shortcut —
        both count. <a href={`${base}/results/`}>See the results →</a>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Scratch slots · memory that is never scored</p>
      <h3>Somewhere to keep intermediate results</h3>
      <p>
        Four memory vectors per question, carried between rounds, lifted multiplication inside mixed training from
        {pct(fam('mix3', 'a*b'))} to {pct(fam('mix3-scratch4', 'a*b'))} and the Fibonacci-like loop from
        {pct(fam('mix3', 'fib(a,a) n15'))} to {pct(fam('mix3-scratch4', 'fib(a,a) n15'))} — but a product followed by
        another step stayed near {pct(fam('mix3-scratch4', 'a*b+b'))}, even when trained on nothing else
        ({pct(fam('nearonly-scratch4', 'a*b+b'))}).
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Bit tokens · step-by-step training tried and dropped</p>
      <h3>One token per answer bit</h3>
      <p>
        The limit was never parameters: carries have to move between bit positions through an intermediate result, and
        one question vector has no structure for that. Giving every answer bit its own token — attending to the other
        bits and to the examples each round — took that capacity check from {pct(fam('nearonly', 'a*b+b'))} to
        <strong>{pct(fam('nearonly-bittoken', 'a*b+b'))}</strong>. Training each round against one intermediate step
        (an operation, or a loop iteration) did not help, kept or faded out: the intermediate values never appear in
        the examples. Bit tokens trained on final answers only became the model. <a href={`${base}/model/`}>The design →</a>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Guardrails · a sealed test set</p>
      <h3>Checking that it is not fitted to our own tests</h3>
      <p>
        Every test had been used to choose architectures and data. A sealed set of 24 everyday functions, written
        separately, is evaluated only at the end of a round and never used to decide anything. Every 8-bit candidate
        cleared it with honest confidence — but at 8 bits the 999 examples cover 1.5% of all inputs, and lookup methods
        that understand nothing answer about a third of it. That, and the next chapter, moved the measure to 16 and 32 bits.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Bit-aligned reading</p>
      <h3>Each answer bit reads the same bit of the examples</h3>
      <p>
        {`On our generator's held-out combinations (T1–T3) it scored ${pct(grp('bt-aligned', 'T1'))} / ${pct(grp('bt-aligned', 'T2'))} / ${pct(grp('bt-aligned', 'T3'))}, 8–16 points above the plain bit tokens — but nothing extra on the sealed set. The gain was specific to relations shaped like our generator's.`}
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Wider numbers</p>
      <h3>One model for any width up to 32 bits</h3>
      <p>
        {`The 8-bit design trained on 12-bit numbers collapsed (hidden compositions ${pct(grp('bt-width12', 'hidden'))}). A width-free version sees every bit relative to its own position and from the lowest bit, so the same weights answer any width up to 32 bits. At 32 bits it gets some unseen relations far above lookup — a conditional it never trained on ${pct(progress.wide.widths['32']?.families['a>b?a-b:a+b'])} against ${pct(progress.wide.widths['32']?.lookup['a>b?a-b:a+b'])} — and fails others completely.`}
        <a href={`${base}/`}>Where it stands →</a>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card result">
      <p class="eyebrow">Composition at 16 bits</p>
      <h3>Identifying the relation, not computing it</h3>
      <p>
        {`It computes a*b alone almost perfectly at 16 bits, but a*b+b was ${pct(fam('wide-ps16', 'a*b+b'))} even after training only on products followed by a step. Trained on just four such relations it gets a*b+b ${pct(fam('wide-near16', 'a*b+b'))}: the arithmetic is fine; recognising which relation the examples come from is not. Deeply worked probe examples (${pct(fam('wide-ps16-peers', 'a*b+b'))}), four layers of per-example processing, and a pool of 16 relations did not change that. The model learns these relations one at a time, each needing about a thousand episodes, instead of one way to compose them.`}
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Mixed training, longer</p>
      <h3>No late jump</h3>
      <p>
        Multiplication at 16 bits had stayed near zero for 7,000 updates of mixed training before rising to 70–80%, so
        a long run trained on every kind of composition at random, at 16, 24 and 32 bits. Six thousand updates in,
        every composition built on a product was still at 0–2% at every check, and it was stopped.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card result">
      <p class="eyebrow">A composition grid</p>
      <h3>It memorises combinations instead of combining parts</h3>
      <p>
        Eight product-like first steps × eight second steps: 52 combinations trained, 12 held out, every part still
        trained in other combinations. The trained combinations it learned reached 87–97%, but held-out ones whose
        parts it knew stayed low:
        {` (a*b)+a ${pct(fam('wide-grid', '(a*b)+a'))} and ((a+1)*b)^b ${pct(fam('wide-grid', '((a+1)*b)^b'))}.`}
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card result">
      <p class="eyebrow">Chains · a new measure</p>
      <h3>Multi-step chains work — except through a product</h3>
      <p>
        Chains are a start such as <code>a+b</code> or <code>a*b</code> followed by one or two of 21 kinds of step, with
        a fresh random constant each time. A new measure counts only the questions every lookup method gets wrong. At
        32 bits the model answers chains of additions, bitwise operations and shifts mostly where lookup fails, and its
        sure answers are always right; chains through a product it hardly ever gets, and it is never sure of them —
        although products followed by a step were in its training. The composition failure of the last chapters was a
        product failure.
        <a href={`${base}/findings/`}>Numbers in the findings →</a>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tried · a relation-code bottleneck</p>
      <h3>Guess the relation first, then apply it</h3>
      <p>
        The answer bits read the 999 examples directly, which lets the model look up similar examples and learn each
        relation on its own. The current experiment adds a bottleneck: a few relation-code tokens per bit position read
        the examples, and the answer bits read only that code. Still one pass, nothing fitted at answer time, and the
        code is free vectors rather than a choice among fixed operations. It learned trained combinations faster, in
        half the time, but combined known parts no better.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where it goes next</h2>
  <div class="grid-3">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>If composing appears</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Scale it: the target is unseen multi-step expressions at 32 bits, such as
        <code>((a*b)^(a&gt;&gt;3))|(b+9)</code>, where even 25% exact could not be luck.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>If it does not</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Then this design memorises compositions instead of generalising them, and needs a structural change — still
        guessing the relation in one pass, never searching or fine-tuning at answer time.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Then: noisy data</h3>
      <p class="small">
        The aim is not a calculator. Once fixed rules can be inferred, the same approach goes to examples that are
        partly corrupted or random, to see which patterns still emerge.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h2 id="experiments">Every experiment</h2>
  <p>
    Exact match on 1,024 fresh questions per relation (64 episodes × 16, seed 60000), at 8 bits unless the run says
    otherwise. "Trained" is the mean over the relations each run trained on, so rows with different training sets are
    not directly comparable. Every run is described in the <a href={`${base}/findings/`}>findings</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="card table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>chapter</th>
          <th>run</th>
          <th class="num">trained</th>
          <th class="num">hidden</th>
          <th class="num">a*b</th>
          <th class="num">a*b+a</th>
          <th class="num">min</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          progress.experiments.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="muted small">{e.chapter}</td>
              <td>{e.title}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(e.groups.train?.exact)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(e.groups.hidden?.exact)}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(e.highlights['a*b'])}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(e.highlights['a*b+a'])}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(e.highlights.min)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .timeline {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .timeline li {
    position: relative;
    margin-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .timeline li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -1.5rem;
    top: 1.5rem;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--axis);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--page);
  }

  .timeline::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 4px;
    top: 1rem;
    bottom: 1rem;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--grid);
  }

  .timeline .pivot::before {
    background: var(--series-2);
  }

  .timeline .result::before {
    background: var(--series-1);
  }

  .timeline h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .timeline table {
    max-width: 520px;
  }

  .pivot,
  .result {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--series-1);
  }

  .pivot {
    border-left-color: var(--series-2);
  }
</style>
